WEBVTT


00:00:01.017 --> 00:00:07.017
Hello, welcome back to our course. Today, we are going to talk about graphics in R.

00:00:06.855 --> 00:00:13.855
Alright, so the very first function to use, once we would like to plot something, is the function plot()

00:00:14.088 --> 00:00:18.088
and then we need to pass it two vectors of the same length.

00:00:17.918 --> 00:00:21.918
For example, here you can see that I passed to it a vector x

00:00:21.574 --> 00:00:28.574
and then a vector y and, as a result, you can see that I have this plot with x on the x-axis

00:00:36.412 --> 00:00:39.412
and y on the y-axis. And here you can see that I also inserted that line par() where I set bg = "grey"

00:00:39.471 --> 00:00:44.471
and this is for a better visibility because of the captions.

00:00:50.051 --> 00:00:52.051
We could also pass a simple two-column data frame or a matrix to plot().

00:00:51.459 --> 00:01:02.459
So here you can see that I loaded the pressure dataset and then I would like to see how the pressure dataset looks like. So a first few lines are temperature

00:01:02.834 --> 00:01:07.834
and pressure and you can see that there are numeric values.

00:01:08.150 --> 00:01:14.150
And now, once I pass it to plot function plot(), you can see that, indeed, on the

00:01:14.055 --> 00:01:23.055
x-axis, I have temperature, on the y-axis, I have pressure and then my curve is actually here, plotted using this

00:01:23.427 --> 00:01:34.427
points. There are many different ways in which I could save my plot, but there is a handy way in R using these graphics devices.

00:01:34.547 --> 00:01:39.547
So here, I could save a plot to a PDF, PNG, or another format

00:01:54.302 --> 00:01:56.302
and then after, basically, asking R to save it, I need to close the device. So here you can see an example, so if I ask to save it to PDF, I need to select the name of the file and then the width and the height of the plot, then I

00:01:56.394 --> 00:02:01.394
select what I would like to save and then I need to close that device.

00:02:01.373 --> 00:02:12.373
There are many different ways we could customize our R plots and then here we could basically pass many parameters.

00:02:22.294 --> 00:02:25.294
For example, we could adjust labels, so here, axes labels, xlab and ylab. We could adjust the size of the labels, using this cex.lab .

00:02:24.408 --> 00:02:30.408
We could also adjust the range of the axes

00:02:30.779 --> 00:02:37.779
using xlim, ylim and there are many different parameters. You can have a look at the help function using this ?par .

00:02:38.445 --> 00:02:45.445
But there are also other customization we could do. For example, we could change the point symbol, colors

00:02:45.664 --> 00:02:52.664
size, etc. For example, to change a point symbol, we use this parameter pch()

00:02:54.961 --> 00:03:03.961
and then we have many symbols, from 0 to 25, then we could change a color, using color(), and then we could also change the size, using this cex

00:03:09.816 --> 00:03:11.816
parameter. We could also add to our plot, to our graphics,

00:03:12.082 --> 00:03:23.082
the title and of course we could change the titles of our axes. So, for example, to add the title to our plot we use this parameter main

00:03:24.642 --> 00:03:29.642
and to adjust the titles of the axes we use xlab and ylab.

00:03:30.136 --> 00:03:36.136
Alternative way to add a title of a plot is just to use the separate function title().

00:03:38.115 --> 00:03:43.115
And, here there are many different types our curve on a plot can look like:

00:03:43.244 --> 00:03:48.244
"p" stands for points (you have already seen it in the very first plot), "l" for

00:03:47.594 --> 00:03:55.594
this solid lines,"b" for both, "h" for histogram (basically vertical bars) etc, etc.

00:03:55.606 --> 00:03:59.606
Okay, let us look at the examples: So here, once again

00:03:59.501 --> 00:04:06.501
I'm plotting this pressure data and now I'm selecting type = "p", so this stands for

00:04:06.993 --> 00:04:11.993
points, once again, and then as my title, I'm selecting main = "pressure data"

00:04:11.515 --> 00:04:17.515
and you can see that now I have my plot with a title.

00:04:17.756 --> 00:04:22.756
If I'd like to change the type of the curve

00:04:22.672 --> 00:04:32.672
from points to a solid line, then I could select a select type = "l" and then you can see that now I can see that solid line.

00:04:38.136 --> 00:04:43.136
We could also select type = "b", which means both points and the lines and then here you can see that I also added

00:04:43.452 --> 00:04:51.452
the title to the plot and I passed the title through that parameter main = "pressure data".

00:04:53.117 --> 00:04:59.117
Here you can see that, instead of a line, I have this vertical bars

00:05:03.160 --> 00:05:06.160
and (if this is something you would fancy) then definitely could choose it.

00:05:12.737 --> 00:05:16.737
And we could also add another symbols and lines to our plot. For example, we could add grid and a legend.

00:05:19.574 --> 00:05:22.574
So if you would like to add vertical and horizontal lines

00:05:22.799 --> 00:05:29.799
we could use this abline(h = seq(.,.,.)) and select the particular vector

00:05:35.583 --> 00:05:40.583
for telling where the line should be. And then we could also use this abline(v = seq(.,.,.)) to add vertical lines. And once again,

00:05:45.375 --> 00:05:47.375
I could pass them through that parameter v and then I could basically tell R at which

00:05:53.316 --> 00:05:57.316
points that vertical line should be added. So here, once again, let us look at the example: So, we have

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:06.000
once again our plot. I passed pressure data, and now the type = "p", so I have points

00:06:06.219 --> 00:06:12.219
and then here you can see that I would like to add this horizontal lines.

00:06:11.898 --> 00:06:23.898
So, I passed to parameter h the sequence from 50 to 200 and then the step size is 50. So we can see that I added horizontal lines

00:06:23.727 --> 00:06:33.727
from 50 to 200, and then the step size is 50, yeah? So we have this horizontal lines added. Now, I would like to also add vertical lines.

00:06:42.637 --> 00:06:44.637
I would like to add vertical lines from 100 to 600 by 100, so you can see that I added the first line, then the second line

00:06:49.997 --> 00:06:55.997
and the third line. And then there is also another way, we could also use this parameter grid( ), where,

00:07:00.879 --> 00:07:04.879
basically, it shows me the grid in my plotting window. So here I added the grid, here you can see this is in this,

00:07:04.954 --> 00:07:11.954
blue color, light blue color, so basically nx and ny

00:07:16.318 --> 00:07:19.318
are to select the number of cells and then here you can see that we will have

00:07:22.116 --> 00:07:27.116
two cells on the this x-dimension, 1 and 2, and then four cells

00:07:30.035 --> 00:07:34.035
for the y-axis: 1, 2, 3, 4.

00:07:34.155 --> 00:07:39.155
And we can also add a legend, so then we are going to see how to add the legend.

00:07:42.341 --> 00:07:48.341
We could also add text to our plotting window. So here, once again, I'm calling my data pressure

00:07:53.900 --> 00:07:55.900
and then I would like to add a line

00:08:01.465 --> 00:08:07.465
but instead of selecting type = "l": How can I do it? I just plot pressure without any parameters

00:08:06.659 --> 00:08:13.659
and then I add this another function, lines(pressure). So you can see that lines(),

00:08:14.036 --> 00:08:22.036
basically, it's for plotting lines in our plotting windows, so here, the default was a plot with

00:08:22.384 --> 00:08:29.384
points and now, by selecting this lines(pressure), I added on top of that

00:08:29.714 --> 00:08:40.714
a line. And then here, I could also add a text, as I said. How can I do it? Well, I select the position of the text. So, the position of the text, the first

00:08:48.537 --> 00:08:52.537
I select X axis, so here, on the x-axis I have 150, okay? And then, on the y-axis, here, we selected 200, so that

00:08:56.039 --> 00:08:58.039
corresponds to that particular place. And what is the label? The label here would be "Hello".

00:09:04.599 --> 00:09:09.599
We could also add mathematical expressions to our plot. We could include symbols in the text and the labels of the plots

00:09:09.652 --> 00:09:15.652
and symbols are available as expressions and there is this handy function called

00:09:27.762 --> 00:09:30.762
bquote(), we could use here. Alright, so now let us define this new vector X and I'm defining it using the sequence function, so I would like to have values from minus 2 to 2 and then the length is 200.

00:09:33.733 --> 00:09:36.733
And then here, I would like to plot on the x-axis x

00:09:36.695 --> 00:09:40.695
and then on the y-axis, I would like to plot density

00:09:40.680 --> 00:09:45.680
of these points and then I'm assuming that these points

00:09:45.866 --> 00:09:54.866
coming from a normal distribution, so I would like to plot the density of a normal distribution and then the type = "l",

00:09:54.694 --> 00:10:03.694
so line. Now, how can I add a text? So here, we define these two parameters, mu is zero, variance is 1.

00:10:10.406 --> 00:10:12.406
And then, I could add text using this text function, so basically first, once again, I need to select the position where

00:10:12.642 --> 00:10:19.642
the text appears, so I would need to find 0 on the x-axis, 0.2 on the y-axis

00:10:20.265 --> 00:10:31.265
and the position tells me the position of the label relative to x and y. So, pos = 1 puts the label just below the point 0, 0.2.

00:10:31.772 --> 00:10:37.772
And then, how can I add the label? So, we use that function bquote() to label.

00:10:37.738 --> 00:10:47.738
Then, I use paste() and then what label I would like to add. I would like to write that there is a "normal density", then I would like to paste it with mu. Mu will be

00:10:47.857 --> 00:10:52.857
a mathematical symbol, mu, and then here we can see that that notation

00:10:52.904 --> 00:11:02.904
with a dot and a mu in parentheses ".(mu)" refers to my mu defined here. So, you'll see in the plot 0

00:11:02.688 --> 00:11:12.688
similarly, for sigma squared, here we would have this sigma squared in the plot as a symbol and then the variance here, you can see

00:11:12.792 --> 00:11:17.792
that would refer to that second parameter. Okay, so how does it look like?

00:11:18.175 --> 00:11:27.175
It looks like this, so indeed, we had these values from minus 2 to 2 on the x-axis,

00:11:26.845 --> 00:11:33.845
the density on the y-axis, then we indeed have a normal density and then

00:11:34.118 --> 00:11:43.118
we selected the place where the text should appear and then our text is "normal density mu=0", and "sigma^2=1".

00:11:43.249 --> 00:11:49.249
Okay, let me stop here and then we are going to come back to graphics in the next video. Thank you!

